<!--
  Gate MP2 appliance settings seed for Kodi on RK3588.

  Only the settings this appliance actually depends on are listed; everything
  else is left at Kodi's defaults so that upgrading Kodi does not silently
  inherit a stale opinion from this file.

  Deliberately NOT set here, because Gate MP2 proved upstream already does the
  right thing and pinning them would only create drift:

    * videoscreen.whitelist - left EMPTY on purpose. With an empty whitelist
      Kodi builds a default one from every mode at or above the GUI resolution,
      so a 1080p GUI still reaches 3840x2160@23.976 for the film. Writing an
      explicit entry would mean guessing Kodi's own refresh-rate formatting.
    * anything about deep colour - the vendor VOP2 already picks a 10-bit bus
      format from the NV15 source; Gate MP1a's a1/a2 rungs show YUYV10_1X20
      before color_depth was ever set.
-->
<settings version="2">
  <!-- Hardware decode to DRM PRIME. This is the switch that makes Kodi look
       for a decoder advertising AV_PIX_FMT_DRM_PRIME, which is how it finds
       hevc_rkmpp. Without it Kodi falls back to software HEVC. -->
  <setting id="videoplayer.useprimedecoderforhw">true</setting>

  <!-- The master switch above is gated on this one. It defaults to false, and
       while it is false useprimedecoderforhw is *disabled* even though it
       reads true, so Kodi silently software-decodes HEVC. Gate MP2 found this
       by inspecting the running instance, not from the log. -->
  <setting id="videoplayer.useprimedecoder">true</setting>

  <!-- 0 = Direct To Plane. The default is 1 (EGL), which imports the decoded
       buffer as a GL texture: every 4K frame would then be handled by the GUI
       renderer instead of being scanned out by the display engine. Direct To
       Plane is what Gate MP1b proved and what keeps NV15 on plane 73. -->
  <setting id="videoplayer.useprimerenderer">0</setting>

  <!-- 2 = ADJUST_REFRESHRATE_ON_STARTSTOP: switch the display to the film's
       own rate when playback starts and back when it stops. A 23.976 film
       pulled to 60 Hz is a Gate MP1b failure, so this is not optional. -->
  <setting id="videoplayer.adjustrefreshrate">2</setting>

  <!-- Kodi gates passthrough on this in CVideoPlayerAudio: allowpassthrough is
       literally !usedisplayasclock, so a true here switches compressed output
       off in code, no matter what the audiooutput settings below say. It is
       false by default and was already false when Gate MA1 started, but it is
       written out explicitly because nothing else in this file hints that a
       *video clock* setting can silence the audio path. -->
  <setting id="videoplayer.usedisplayasclock">false</setting>

  <!-- The HDMI sink, named rather than left on "Default". Both resolve to the
       same card today, but Kodi rewrites an unresolvable device setting to its
       own default, and this appliance re-enumerates its audio devices every
       time the display mode changes. Naming the endpoint means a rewrite is
       visible in the diff instead of silently moving the audio somewhere else
       -- the board also has an ES8323 analogue codec that would happily take
       the stream. The string is Kodi's own, taken from the option list it
       publishes for this machine.

       Gate MA1 moved both of these off "sysdefault" and onto the "hdmi" PCM
       that config/alsa/rockchip-hdmi1.conf adds. That PCM is the only one Kodi
       will ever consider for passthrough -- CAESinkALSA::AEDeviceTypeFromName()
       decides AE_DEVTYPE_HDMI purely from the name's "hdmi" prefix -- and it is
       also what applies the AES channel-status bytes per stream: 0x04 for PCM,
       0x06 (non-audio) for a compressed burst. Pointing the PCM device at it
       too keeps one device for both jobs, so switching between a GUI sound and
       a film does not re-open a different endpoint mid-playback. -->
  <!-- Rendered on every start by packaging/mediabox-hdmi-prepare, for the
       sound card that belongs to the display output this box is on. On a board
       with two HDMI sockets that is not the same card in both. -->
  <setting id="audiooutput.audiodevice">@ALSA_DEVICE@</setting>
  <setting id="audiooutput.passthroughdevice">@ALSA_DEVICE@</setting>

  <!-- Compressed passthrough, validated in Gate MA1 against this sink's ELD
       rather than against what Kodi is willing to offer. CAESinkALSA pushes
       every stream type it can do onto an HDMI device on purpose ("we don't
       trust ELD information"), so the UI lists TrueHD and DTS-HD here as well;
       neither appears in the sink's SADs and both stay off.

       What the ELD actually declares: LPCM 6ch, AC-3 6ch/640k, DTS 6ch/1504k,
       E-AC-3 8ch. What the sink actually reproduces, measured at the TV:
       AC-3 and E-AC-3 only. DTS and 6-channel LPCM are declared and silent,
       so DTS passthrough is off and the PCM path stays at 2.0 -- a DTS title
       is decoded and downmixed rather than sent as something that plays as
       nothing.

       ac3transcode is off for the same reason: with it on, a DTS source is
       re-encoded (to E-AC-3, or to AC-3 when eac3passthrough is off) and this
       sink produced silence from both, while the stream Kodi built was clean
       at every machine-visible layer -- same 48 kHz S16_LE carrier and same
       AES0=0x06 as the AC-3 passthrough that does work. That is its own fault
       domain and not something MA1 settled. -->
  <setting id="audiooutput.passthrough">true</setting>
  <setting id="audiooutput.ac3passthrough">true</setting>
  <setting id="audiooutput.eac3passthrough">true</setting>
  <setting id="audiooutput.dtspassthrough">false</setting>
  <setting id="audiooutput.ac3transcode">false</setting>
  <setting id="audiooutput.truehdpassthrough">false</setting>
  <setting id="audiooutput.dtshdpassthrough">false</setting>
  <setting id="audiooutput.channels">1</setting>

  <!-- JSON-RPC over HTTP, so this headless appliance can be driven and
       measured without a keyboard on the TV. -->
  <setting id="services.webserver">true</setting>
  <setting id="services.webserverport">8080</setting>
  <setting id="services.webserverauthentication">false</setting>
  <setting id="services.esenabled">true</setting>
</settings>
